WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.522
Grouping becomes much more useful when we calculate several summary measures at the same time.

00:00:06.522 --> 00:00:09.362
Here, we group the orders by region.

00:00:09.362 --> 00:00:16.852
For each region, we calculate total sales, the average order amount, and the number of orders.

00:00:16.852 --> 00:00:21.417
Notice that we also give each output column a meaningful name.

00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:27.522
This is called named aggregation, and it produces a report that is much easier to read than a table with vague or

00:00:27.522 --> 00:00:31.488
automatically generated column names.

00:00:32.016 --> 00:00:35.223
Think about how common this pattern is in real work.

00:00:35.223 --> 00:00:43.118
A department report might need total expenses, average transaction size, and the number of transactions.

00:00:43.118 --> 00:00:50.188
A healthcare report might need the number of visits, average length of stay, and total charges.

00:00:50.188 --> 00:00:56.973
A customer report might need total purchases, average purchase amount, and order count.

00:00:56.973 --> 00:01:00.368
Run the example and compare the regions.

00:01:00.368 --> 00:01:03.543
Then try grouping by channel instead of region.

00:01:03.543 --> 00:01:08.306
The syntax stays almost the same, but the analytical question changes.
